1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:05,019
Exoplanet Finder is a PyTorch model that scores possible planet signals

2
00:00:05,019 --> 00:00:07,547
in NASA Kepler light curves.

3
00:00:07,547 --> 00:00:12,013
Here's Kepler-90 i, a known planet we use as a demo.

4
00:00:12,013 --> 00:00:14,080
Four years of Kepler data:

5
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:18,380
the planet's dip is only about 116 parts per million.

6
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:23,847
Folding at the orbital period stacks 74 transits, and the dip appears.

7
00:00:23,847 --> 00:00:28,213
The network reads the whole orbit, which can reveal eclipsing binaries,

8
00:00:28,213 --> 00:00:30,497
and a close-up of the dip.

9
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:38,515
It scores 0.987, above the validation threshold of 0.671:

10
00:00:38,515 --> 00:00:40,999
it resembles labelled planet candidates.

11
00:00:40,999 --> 00:00:44,313
That's not a probability, or a confirmation.

12
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:48,562
The DR24 training labels call this signal a false positive,

13
00:00:48,562 --> 00:00:51,663
yet it scores 0.999.

14
00:00:51,663 --> 00:00:54,949
Later DR25 vetting calls it a candidate,

15
00:00:54,949 --> 00:01:00,893
and NASA's Exoplanet Archive lists it as confirmed planet Kepler-529 d.

16
00:01:00,893 --> 00:01:03,863
The benchmark still counts it as an error.

17
00:01:03,863 --> 00:01:09,563
On 2,362 signals from 1,481 unseen stars,

18
00:01:09,563 --> 00:01:14,184
the CNN reaches an average precision of 0.913,

19
00:01:14,184 --> 00:01:19,013
versus 0.873 for a gradient-boosting baseline.

20
00:01:19,013 --> 00:01:22,813
The code, trained model and evaluation are on GitHub.

21
00:01:22,813 --> 00:01:24,508
Thanks to NASA's Kepler mission

22
00:01:24,508 --> 00:01:28,273
and to Shallue and Vanderburg's AstroNet research.

